what is the impact of war in ukraine on  the economy and indeed the cost of  living well we've just got the verdict  from the oecd major economic body and  how the world is doing and i'm afraid  it's a slightly depressing story this  shows you global economic growth  forecast that they had last time around  so back in december this is what they  were forecasting for this year and for  next year but here's how those numbers  have just changed in their latest  forecast look at those bars latest  forecasts down both of them down  significantly especially this year three  percent this year 2.8 percent next year  for global growth that's actually the  weakest since 2009 so the weakest since  the financial crisis as a result of what  we're going through right now and if you  look specifically at all of the  different major countries the oecd is  basically a club of developed developed  economies so if you look at all of those  oecd nations including the uk there's  the uk in the middle the red bar have a  look at this this is this year the  forecast for this year's growth uk's  middle of the pack look at next year  we're going to just go through next year  look at the red bar down down down down  actually the uk with the weakest  economic growth of any oecd country in  2023 raising a question why is that well  here's the chief economist of the oecd  the uk  specific  again are  a very fast pace of monetary tightening  like in the u.s  a very  rapid fiscal consolidation  uh the seven after the u.s in the g7 but  the u.s had wider deficit  um and then  the brexit which is adding to this with  threat frictions so  all of these are obviously impacting  uk firms and citizens  so brexit but also extra fiscal  consolidation what does that mean it  means basically the uk is starting to do  a kind of austerity cutting back and the  oecd says it needs to potentially uh do  a little bit less of that but here's one  other thing that's worth pointing out  the gap between what richer and poorer  households are feeling in terms of the  cost of living squeeze well the red bars  here show you the inflation rate on food  and energy for those in low-income  households the blue bars are those those  in high-income households look the red  bars are a lot higher than the blue bars  but in particular in the uk in fact that  gap there between red and blue  low-income and high-income households is  the highest in the uk of any of these  countries  around the world just underlining that  for all the talk about leveling up there  are still  some very big gaps out there
 

